ure, Mamba achieves state-of-the-art results on a diverse set of domains, where it matches or exceeds the performance of strong Transformer models. We are excited about the broad applications of selective state space models to build foundation models for different domains, especially in emerging modalities requiring long context such as genomics, audio, and video. Our results suggest that Mamba is a strong candidate to be a general sequence model backbone.\n\n###### Acknowledgments\n\nWe thank Karan Goel, Arjun Desai, and Kush Bhatia for helpful feedback on the draft.","original_chars":791},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"A Simplified SSM Architecture","heading_level":3,"line_start":182,"line_end":187,"text":"As with structured SSMs, selective SSMs are standalone sequence transformations that can be flexibly incorporated into neural networks. The H3 architecture is the basis for the most well-known SSM architectures (Section 2), which are generally comprised of a block inspired by linear attention interleaved with an MLP (multi-layer perceptron) block. We simplify this architecture by combining these two components into one, which is stacked homogenously (Figure 3). This is inspired by the gated attention unit (GAU) (Hua et al. 2022), which did something similar for attention.\n\nThis architecture involves expanding the model dimension 𝐷 by a controllable expansion factor 𝐸 . For each block, most of the parameters (3 𝐸𝐷 2 ) are in the linear projections (2 𝐸𝐷 2 for input projections, 𝐸𝐷 2 for output projection) while the inner SSM contributes less. The number of SSM parameters (projections for Δ , 𝑩 , 𝑪 , and the matrix 𝑨 ) are much smaller in comparison. We repeat this block, interleaved with standard normalization and residual connections, to form the Mamba architecture. We always fix to 𝐸 = 2 in our experiments and use two stacks of the block to match the 12 𝐷 2 parameters of a Transformer's interleaved MHA (multi-head attention) and MLP blocks. Weuse the SiLU / Swish activation function (Hendrycks and Gimpel 2016; Ramachandran, Zoph, and Quoc V Le 2017), motivated so that the Gated MLP becomes the popular 'SwiGLU' variant (Chowdhery et al. 2023; Dauphin et al. 2017; Shazeer 2020; Touvron et al. 2023). [truncated]","original_chars":1739},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Architecture","heading_level":4,"line_start":421,"line_end":478,"text":"Table 6 investigates the effects of the architecture (block) and its inner SSM layer (Figure 3). We find that\n\n- Among previous non-selective (LTI) SSMs, which are equivalent to global convolutions, performance is very similar.\n- Replacing the complex-valued S4 variant from previous work with a real-valued one does not affect performance much, suggesting that (at least for LM) real-valued SSMs may be a better choice when accounting for hardware efficiency.\n- Replacing any of these with a selective SSM (S6) significantly improves performance, validating the motivation of Section 3.\n\nTable 5: ( SC09 Model Ablations ) Models with 6M parameters. In SaShiMi's U-Net backbone, there are 8 center blocks operating on sequence length 1000, sandwiched on each side by 8 outer blocks on sequence length 4000, sandwiched by 8 outer blocks on sequence length 16000 (40 blocks total). The architecture of the 8 center blocks are ablated independently of the rest. Note that Transformers (MHA+MLP) were not tested in the more important outer blocks because of efficiency constraints.\n\n| Outer | Center | NLL ↓ | FID ↓ | IS ↑ | mIS ↑ | AM ↓ |\n|---------|----------|---------|---------|--------|---------|--------|\n| S4+MLP | MHA+MLP | 1.859 | 1.45 | 5.06 | 47.03 | 0.70 |\n| S4+MLP | S4+MLP | 1.867 | 1.43 | 5.42 | 53.54 | 0.65 |\n| S4+MLP | Mamba | 1.859 | 1.42 | 5.71 | 56.51 | 0.64 |\n| Mamba | MHA+MLP | 1.850 | 1.37 | 5.63 | 58.23 | 0.62 |\n| Mamba | S4+MLP | 1.853 | 1.07 | 6.05 | 73.34 | 0.55 |\n| Mamba | Mamba | 1.852 | 0.94 | 6.26 | 88.54 | 0.52 | [truncated]","original_chars":3491}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001774.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001774","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary model paper proposing Mamba, not a review or wrapper. It includes both language and genomics as modalities, but the provided evidence does not describe a substantive text-bio bridge or text-conditioned biological generation.","evidence_snippet":"\"performance across several modalities such as language, audio, and genomics\"; \"general sequence model backbone\"; \"discrete modalities\"; \"emerging modalities requiring long context such as genomics\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001774","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a primary model paper introducing Mamba as a new sequence model backbone. The record supports foundation-model-like breadth and pretraining, but it does not explicitly state a decoder, autoregressive, seq2seq, or other clearly generative mechanism in the provided text.","evidence_snippet":"\"selective SSMs into a simplified end-to-end neural network architecture without attention or even MLP blocks (Mamba)\"; \"large models pretrained on massive data then adapted for downstream tasks\"; \"build foundation models for different domains\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

